          "A) 'Pyt'",
          "B) 'yth' (*)",
          "C) 'ytho'",
          "D) 'Pyth'",
          "Explanation: Slicing [1:4] returns indices 1, 2, 3 — 'y', 't', 'h' — because stop is exclusive.",
          "Q2: Are strings mutable in Python?",
          "A) Yes, you can do s[0] = 'x'",
          "B) Only in Python 3.12+",
          "C) Only byte strings are mutable",
          "D) No, strings are immutable; modifications return new strings (*)",
          "Explanation: str objects cannot be changed after creation; \"modifying\" a string creates a new one.",
          "Q3: Which is the most Pythonic way to format `\"Ada is 36\"` from name=\"Ada\", age=36?",
          "A) f\"{name} is {age}\" (*)",
          "B) \"{0} is {1}\".format(name, age)",
          "C) \"%s is %d\" % (name, age)",
          "D) name + \" is \" + str(age)",
          "Explanation: f-strings (3.6+) are the recommended, fastest, and most readable option.",
          "Q4: What is `b\"hello\"[0]`?",
          "A) 'h'",
          "B) \"h\"",
          "C) 104 (*)",
          "D) b'h'",
          "Explanation: Indexing bytes returns an int (the byte value); 'h' is ASCII 104.",
          "Q5: Which method removes leading/trailing whitespace?",
          "A) trim()",
          "B) strip() (*)",
          "C) clean()",
          "D) chop()",
          "Explanation: strip() removes leading/trailing whitespace (or chars in the given set); lstrip/rstrip do one side only.",
          "Q6: What does `\",\".join([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])` return?",
          "A) 'abc'",
          "B) ',abc'",
          "C) ['a,b,c']",
          "D) 'a,b,c' (*)",
          "Explanation: join() concatenates the iterable's items with the separator between each pair: 'a,b,c'.",
          "Q7: What does `\"hello\".replace(\"l\", \"L\")` produce?",
          "A) 'heLLo' (*)",
          "B) 'heLlo'",
          "C) 'HeLLo'",
          "D) 'hello'",
          "Explanation: replace() substitutes ALL occurrences by default — both l's become L's.",
          "Q8: How do you reverse a string s?",
          "A) s.reverse()",
          "B) reversed(s)",
          "C) s[::-1] (*)",
          "D) s[-1:0]",
          "Explanation: s[::-1] uses slice notation with step -1, returning the reversed string.",
          "Q9: What's the issue with `\"500px\".strip(\"px\")`?",
          "A) Raises ValueError",
          "B) Returns '500' — looks right but it stripped ALL leading/trailing 'p' and 'x' chars, not the literal \"px\" (*)",
          "C) Returns '500px' unchanged",
          "D) Returns '500p'",
          "Explanation: strip() removes any char in the set {'p','x'} from the ends, not a substring; \"x500px\" would also become \"500\".",
          "Q10: What's the recommended way to build a large string from many parts?",
          "A) Use io.StringIO or \"\".join(parts) (*)",
          "B) Repeated s += chunk in a loop",
          "C) Use list.insert at index 0",
          "D) Print each part to a file",
          "Explanation: Repeated += is O(n^2); join() builds the final string in one pass.",
          "----------------------------------------------------------------------"
