          "Create a tuple `[string, number, boolean]`, then attempt to `push` an extra element — observe the warning and apply `as const` or `readonly`.",
          "Enable `noImplicitAny` in tsconfig and refactor any parameters that were implicitly `any`.",
          ">>> QUIZ (Stage 2) <<<",
          "(Z AI: render this as an interactive multiple-choice quiz in the Learn tab)",
          "Q1: What does `const x = \"hi\"` infer as?",
          "A) `string`",
          "B) `\"hi\"` (literal type) (*)",
          "C) `unknown`",
          "D) `any`",
          "Explanation: `const` bindings are non-widening, so TS infers the literal type `\"hi\"` rather than the broader `string`.",
          "Q2: Which type accepts any value but requires narrowing before use?",
          "A) `any`",
          "B) `object`",
          "C) `unknown` (*)",
          "D) `never`",
          "Explanation: `unknown` is the type-safe counterpart to `any` — assignable from anything but you must narrow it before operating on it.",
          "Q3: What is the inferred type of `let n = 42;`?",
          "A) `42`",
          "B) `Number`",
          "C) `int`",
          "D) `number` (*)",
          "Explanation: `let` allows reassignment, so TS widens the literal `42` to the base type `number`.",
          "Q4: Which annotation declares a tuple of a string and a number?",
          "A) `[string, number]` (*)",
          "B) `(string, number)`",
          "C) `{string, number}`",
          "D) `<string, number>`",
          "Explanation: TS uses square brackets with comma-separated element types for tuples: `[string, number]`.",
          "Q5: Which is the correct type for an array of numbers?",
          "A) `number{}`",
          "B) `Array<number>` and `number[]` are both correct (*)",
          "C) `list<number>`",
          "D) `[number]`",
          "Explanation: Both `number[]` and `Array<number>` denote an array of numbers; the former is preferred for brevity.",
          "Q6: What does `noImplicitAny` do?",
          "A) Forbids the explicit `any` keyword",
          "B) Converts all `any` to `unknown`",
          "C) Reports an error when a type would otherwise be inferred as `any` (*)",
          "D) Disables the `any` type entirely",
          "Explanation: `noImplicitAny` causes `tsc` to error on parameters/variables whose type cannot be inferred and would silently become `any`.",
