          "Wrap two UPDATEs in a transaction; deliberately throw an exception to verify the rollback.",
          "Use `FETCH_CLASS` to hydrate a `User` object from a SELECT; verify the constructor runs after property assignment.",
          "Demonstrate that identifiers can't be parameterized by trying to bind `:sort` for an `ORDER BY` clause — then fix it with an allow-list.",
          ">>> QUIZ (Stage 14) <<<",
          "(Z AI: render this as an interactive multiple-choice quiz in the Learn tab)",
          "Q1: Which method prepares a SQL statement with bound parameters?",
          "A) `PDO::query()`",
          "B) `PDO::prepare()` (*)",
          "C) `PDO::exec()`",
          "D) `PDO::run()`",
          "Explanation: `PDO::prepare()` returns a `PDOStatement` that you execute with bound parameters, separating SQL structure from data — preventing injection.",
          "Q2: What does `ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES = false` do?",
          "A) Disables prepared statements",
          "B) Speeds up queries",
          "C) Uses native driver prepares (more secure) (*)",
          "D) Enables transactions",
          "Explanation: With `false`, PDO uses the database's native prepared statements (more secure, type-correct). With `true` (default for MySQL), it simulates them client-side via interpolation.",
          "Q3: Which error mode throws `PDOException` on errors?",
          "A) `ERRMODE_SILENT`",
          "B) `ERRMODE_WARNING`",
          "C) `ERRMODE_FATAL`",
          "D) `ERRMODE_EXCEPTION` (*)",
          "Explanation: `ERRMODE_EXCEPTION` throws exceptions on errors, letting you handle them with try/catch. `ERRMODE_WARNING` emits E_WARNING; `ERRMODE_SILENT` requires manual `errorCode()` checks.",
          "Q4: Which fetch mode returns an associative array?",
          "A) `FETCH_ASSOC` (*)",
          "B) `FETCH_NUM`",
          "C) `FETCH_BOTH`",
          "D) `FETCH_OBJ`",
          "Explanation: `FETCH_ASSOC` returns arrays keyed by column name. `FETCH_NUM` keys by index; `FETCH_BOTH` includes both; `FETCH_OBJ` returns stdClass instances.",
          "Q5: Why can't you parameterize table or column names?",
          "A) It's a security risk",
          "B) PDO doesn't support placeholders for identifiers — they bind *values* (literals) only (*)",
          "C) It's slower",
          "D) It only works in MySQL",
          "Explanation: Prepared statement placeholders bind values (literals), not identifiers; the SQL structure is compiled first. Validate identifiers with an allow-list and interpolate.",
          "Q6: What does `beginTransaction()` + `commit()` provide?",
          "A) Faster queries",
          "B) Persistent connections",
          "C) Atomicity — all-or-nothing for multi-statement operations (*)",
          "D) Encryption",
          "Explanation: Transactions wrap multiple statements so they all commit or all roll back — providing atomicity. Use `rollBack()` on exception to undo partial work.",
          "Q7: Which method returns the last inserted auto-increment ID?",
          "A) `PDOStatement::lastId()`",
          "B) `PDO::insertId()`",
          "C) `PDOStatement::rowCount()`",
          "D) `PDO::lastInsertId()` (*)",
          "Explanation: `PDO::lastInsertId()` returns the last auto-increment ID for the current connection. For Postgres sequences, pass the sequence name as an argument.",
          "Q8: Which fetch mode hydrates objects of a specified class?",
          "A) `FETCH_CLASS` (*)",
          "B) `FETCH_OBJ`",
          "C) `FETCH_LAZY`",
          "D) `FETCH_NAMED`",
          "Explanation: `FETCH_CLASS` instantiates the named class and assigns public properties from columns; the constructor runs AFTER property assignment (useful for normalization).",
          "Q9: What does `PDOStatement::fetchColumn()` return?",
          "A) All rows from the result set",
          "B) A single value from the next row's first column (or specified column) (*)",
          "C) The column count",
          "D) An associative array",
          "Explanation: `fetchColumn($col = 0)` returns a single value from the next row's specified column — perfect for `SELECT COUNT(*)` or scalar lookups.",
          "Q10: Why is `rowCount()` unreliable for SELECT on Postgres?",
          "A) It's not implemented",
          "B) It returns the number of columns",
          "C) It returns 0 unless rows have been fetched (Postgres uses server-side cursors) (*)",
          "D) It always returns 1",
          "Explanation: Postgres's `rowCount()` for SELECT returns the number of rows fetched so far (often 0 before fetching). Use `SELECT COUNT(*)` with `fetchColumn()` for reliable counts.",
          "----------------------------------------------------------------------"
