          "Add retry with exponential backoff to a task that calls an external API.",
          "Use chain() to run three tasks sequentially; verify ordering.",
          "Schedule a Beat task to run every 5 minutes and verify with `celery -A mysite beat`.",
          "Set CELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER=True in tests and assert the task ran synchronously.",
          ">>> QUIZ (Stage 14) <<<",
          "(Z AI: render this as an interactive multiple-choice quiz in the Learn tab)",
          "Q1: Which method enqueues a Celery task asynchronously?",
          "A) task.run()",
          "B) task.delay() (*)",
          "C) task.start()",
          "D) task.queue()",
          "Explanation: .delay(*args) is a shortcut for .apply_async(args=...). It returns an AsyncResult immediately.",
          "Q2: What does @shared_task do?",
          "A) Shares the task across users",
          "B) Makes the task thread-safe",
          "C) Registers the function as a Celery task without needing the app instance directly (*)",
          "D) Shares the task between Django and Flask",
          "Explanation: @shared_task creates a task that's registered with whatever Celery app is current. Useful for reusable apps.",
          "Q3: Why should you pass IDs (not model instances) to tasks?",
          "A) IDs are smaller",
          "B) Pickling is deprecated",
          "C) IDs support retries",
          "D) Instances get pickled and become stale; re-fetching inside the task is safer (*)",
          "Explanation: A model instance pickled at enqueue time may be outdated by the time the worker runs. Pass the PK and re-fetch.",
          "Q4: What does app.autodiscover_tasks() do?",
          "A) Loads tasks.py from each INSTALLED_APP automatically (*)",
          "B) Auto-creates tasks from models",
          "C) Discovers existing celery workers",
          "D) Imports all Python files",
          "Explanation: autodiscover_tasks scans each app for a tasks.py module and registers its @shared_task functions.",
          "Q5: What's the difference between soft and hard time limits?",
          "A) Soft is for dev; hard is for prod",
          "B) Soft raises a catchable exception; hard kills the worker process (*)",
          "C) Both kill the process",
          "D) Hard limit is configurable; soft is fixed",
          "Explanation: soft_time_limit raises SoftTimeLimitExceeded (you can catch and clean up). time_limit sends SIGKILL — no cleanup.",
          "Q6: Which Celery construct runs tasks one after another with the output of one feeding the next?",
          "A) group",
          "B) chord",
          "C) chain (*)",
          "D) fanout",
          "Explanation: chain(t1.s(), t2.s(), t3.s()) runs t1 -> t2 -> t3 sequentially; each task's return value is the first arg of the next.",
          "Q7: What does Celery Beat do?",
          "A) Beats (load-balances) workers",
          "B) Monitors task performance",
          "C) Acts as a result backend",
          "D) Schedules periodic tasks on a schedule (crontab) (*)",
          "Explanation: celery beat is a separate process that submits tasks on a schedule defined in CELERY_BEAT_SCHEDULE.",
          "Q8: How do you make a task idempotent?",
          "A) Use a dedup key/row to detect re-runs and skip if already processed (*)",
          "B) Use @idempotent decorator",
          "C) Disable retries",
          "D) Set always_commit=True",
          "Explanation: Create a unique marker (e.g., a Notification row keyed on post_id + kind). If it exists, skip. Same input -> same final state.",
          "Q9: What's the recommended test setting for Celery?",
          "A) Mock every task",
          "B) Set CELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER=True to run tasks inline (*)",
          "C) Don't test Celery",
          "D) Run a real broker in CI",
          "Explanation: ALWAYS_EAGER runs tasks synchronously in the same process — no broker needed. For integration tests, run a real broker.",
          "Q10: Which is the most common broker for Celery in production?",
          "A) RabbitMQ only",
          "B) PostgreSQL",
          "C) Redis or RabbitMQ (Redis is simpler; RabbitMQ is more feature-rich) (*)",
          "D) Kafka",
          "Explanation: Redis is the simplest broker and good for most cases. RabbitMQ adds features like priority queues and better ack semantics for some workloads.",
          "----------------------------------------------------------------------"
