          "Q1: Which function sanitizes an uploaded filename?",
          "A) werkzeug.utils.clean_filename",
          "B) os.path.basename",
          "C) shutil.safe_name",
          "D) werkzeug.utils.secure_filename (*)",
          "Explanation: secure_filename strips path separators and unsafe characters, turning '../../etc/passwd' into 'etc_passwd'.",
          "Q2: Which config key rejects oversized request bodies with 413?",
          "A) MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH (*)",
          "B) MAX_BODY_SIZE",
          "C) BODY_SIZE_LIMIT",
          "D) UPLOAD_MAX_SIZE",
          "Explanation: app.config['MAX_CONTENT_LENGTH'] = N bytes makes Flask reject larger bodies with 413 before reading them.",
          "Q3: Which request attribute holds FileStorage objects for uploads?",
          "A) request.form",
          "B) request.files (*)",
          "C) request.data",
          "D) request.uploads",
          "Explanation: request.files is a MultiDict of FileStorage objects (one per <input type=file>); each has .save(path), .filename, .stream.",
          "Q4: How do you safely parse a JSON body that might be missing?",
          "A) request.json()",
          "B) request.body.json()",
          "C) request.get_json(silent=True) (*)",
          "D) json.loads(request.data)",
          "Explanation: request.get_json(silent=True) returns None instead of raising a 400 when the body is empty or not JSON; default the result with `or {}`.",
          "Q5: How does Flask serve /static in development?",
          "A) Via Nginx",
          "B) Via a CDN",
          "C) It doesn't; you must add a route",
          "D) Via a built-in static view registered on the app (*)",
          "Explanation: Flask auto-registers a static view at /static/<filename> serving from the package's static/ folder. In production use Whitenoise or Nginx.",
          "Q6: Where should user-uploaded files be saved?",
          "A) Outside the package, in a configured upload folder or S3 (*)",
          "B) Inside the package's static/ folder",
          "C) Inside the package's templates/ folder",
          "D) In /tmp",
          "Explanation: Save uploads outside the package root (e.g. instance_path or S3) so redeploys don't wipe them and the package stays read-only.",
          "Q7: Which URL helper generates the URL to static/css/app.css?",
          "A) static_url('css/app.css')",
          "B) url_for('static', filename='css/app.css') (*)",
          "C) url_for('/static/css/app.css')",
          "D) asset('css/app.css')",
          "Explanation: url_for('static', filename='...') resolves to /static/<filename> (or a versioned URL when using a CDN-aware helper).",
          "Q8: What's the correct way to send a saved file to the client?",
          "A) open(path).read()",
          "B) redirect(file://path)",
          "C) send_from_directory(folder, name) (*)",
          "D) Response(open(path))",
          "Explanation: send_from_directory safely serves a file from a directory, preventing path traversal; pair with as_attachment=True to force download.",
          "Q9: What enctype must an upload form use?",
          "A) application/x-www-form-urlencoded",
          "B) text/plain",
          "C) application/json",
          "D) multipart/form-data (*)",
          "Explanation: File uploads require enctype=\"multipart/form-data\" so the browser segments files; otherwise request.files will be empty.",
          "Q10: Why is serving uploads from app.py a bad idea in production?",
          "A) It blocks WSGI workers and risks path-traversal; use S3 + CDN or send_from_directory (*)",
          "B) Browsers block uploads from Python servers",
          "C) Flask cannot return binary data",
          "D) It requires admin privileges",
          "Explanation: Blocking WSGI workers on file I/O kills concurrency, and naive open()/read() invites path traversal; offload to S3 or use send_from_directory.",
