          "A) @keyup.enter (*)",
          "B) @keyup.return",
          "C) @keyup.13",
          "D) @keyup.ENTER",
          "Explanation: Vue provides `.enter` as an alias for the Enter key; `.13` (the keyCode) is deprecated and discouraged.",
          "Q7: What does @click.ctrl.exact require?",
          "A) Ctrl+any other modifier",
          "B) Ctrl only — no other system modifiers pressed (*)",
          "C) Any modifier",
          "D) Just any click",
          "Explanation: `.exact` requires that no other system modifiers (shift/alt/meta) are pressed, so `@click.ctrl.exact` means Ctrl alone.",
          "Q8: How are key modifier names derived?",
          "A) From event.keyCode",
          "B) From event.code only",
          "C) From event.key kebab-cased (*)",
          "D) Arbitrary names",
          "Explanation: Vue derives key modifier names from `KeyboardEvent.key` kebab-cased, e.g. `PageDown` -> `@keyup.page-down`.",
          "Q9: Which is TRUE about combining .passive with .prevent?",
          "A) They work great together",
          "B) .prevent wins",
          "C) .passive wins",
          "D) It's contradictory — .passive hints no preventDefault, .prevent calls it; Vue warns (*)",
          "Explanation: `.passive` and `.prevent` contradict each other; Vue will warn and the browser will ignore the preventDefault call.",
          "Q10: What does @click.self do?",
          "A) Calls the handler only if event.target is the element itself, not a descendant (*)",
          "B) Calls the handler only on the root element",
          "C) Prevents propagation",
          "D) Runs the handler once",
          "Explanation: `.self` only triggers the handler if `event.target === event.currentTarget`, ignoring bubbled events from descendants.",
          "----------------------------------------------------------------------"
