          "Q1: What is a Vue composable?",
          "A) A Vue plugin",
          "B) A function that encapsulates reactive logic using Vue's reactivity APIs (*)",
          "C) A SFC component",
          "D) A type of directive",
          "Explanation: A composable is a function (conventionally prefixed `use`) that uses ref/computed/watch/lifecycle hooks to encapsulate and reuse reactive logic.",
          "Q2: Why should composables return refs (not plain values)?",
          "A) Refs are faster",
          "B) TypeScript requires it",
          "C) Plain values snapshot at call time and lose reactivity when destructured; refs preserve it (*)",
          "D) Plain values are not allowed",
          "Explanation: Returning refs lets consumers destructure them and stay reactive; returning a plain value snapshots the value at call time.",
          "Q3: What naming convention do Vue composables follow?",
          "A) Prefix with `get`",
          "B) Suffix with `Composable`",
          "C) CamelCase only",
          "D) Prefix with `use` (e.g. useMouse) (*)",
          "Explanation: Vue convention is to prefix composables with `use`, mirroring the React hooks convention; the Vue tooling and docs assume this.",
          "Q4: What does toRefs() do?",
          "A) Converts each property of a reactive object into a ref so they can be destructured without losing reactivity (*)",
          "B) Converts a ref to a reactive object",
          "C) Removes reactivity",
          "D) Converts a ref to a plain value",
          "Explanation: `toRefs(reactiveObj)` returns an object whose properties are refs linked to the original, enabling safe destructuring.",
          "Q5: How does an async composable integrate with the parent?",
          "A) It just works",
          "B) The parent must wrap the component in <Suspense> (*)",
          "C) You pass a callback",
          "D) Async composables are not supported",
          "Explanation: A composable using top-level `await` makes setup async; the parent must wrap the component in `<Suspense>` with a fallback template.",
          "Q6: What does onCleanup() do inside a watchEffect?",
          "A) Cancels the effect",
          "B) Resets the deps",
          "C) Registers a cleanup function that runs before the next effect run and on scope disposal (*)",
          "D) Disables the effect",
          "Explanation: `onCleanup(fn)` (imported from `vue`) registers a function that runs before the next effect run and when the scope is disposed — ideal for aborting fetches.",
          "Q7: What is effectScope for?",
          "A) Creating a new Vue app",
          "B) Defining computed scopes",
          "C) A replacement for setup",
          "D) Grouping effects so they can be stopped together outside of a component (*)",
          "Explanation: `effectScope()` creates a scope that collects effects created inside it; calling `.stop()` disposes them all — useful for stores, plugins, and tests.",
          "Q8: Where can lifecycle hooks (onMounted, etc.) be called?",
          "A) Only synchronously during a component's setup (or a composable called from it) (*)",
          "B) Anywhere",
          "C) Inside async functions",
          "D) Inside <template>",
          "Explanation: Lifecycle hooks register with the current component instance captured during setup; calling them outside setup (or after an `await`) silently fails.",
          "Q9: What does readonly() return when wrapping a ref?",
          "A) A plain value",
          "B) A new ref that warns on mutation in dev (*)",
          "C) The same ref",
          "D) An array",
          "Explanation: `readonly(ref)` returns a proxy ref that warns when you try to set `.value` — useful for exposing immutable state from composables.",
          "Q10: Which is a built-in Vue 3 composable for unique IDs?",
          "A) useUuid()",
          "B) useRandom()",
          "C) useId() (*)",
          "D) useHash()",
          "Explanation: `useId()` (Vue 3.5+) generates a stable, SSR-safe unique ID for a11y attributes like `aria-labelledby` and `for`/`id` pairs.",
          "----------------------------------------------------------------------"
